{
  "concept": "In Graham's scan, the turn direction at the top of the stack decides hull membership: a left turn extends the hull to include the new point, while a nonleft turn proves the middle point lies inside a triangle of other points and must be popped.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Panel (a) label beneath the left figure, panel (b) label beneath the right figure",
    "Panel (a): light gray filled convex polygon Q_j with interior text label 'Q_j'",
    "Panel (a): vertex p_0 at bottom left (black dot, label below-left)",
    "Panel (a): vertex p_j at top (black dot, label above)",
    "Panel (a): vertex p_k to the right of p_j (black dot, label above-right)",
    "Panel (a): two unlabeled black dots along the upper-right boundary chain",
    "Panel (a): vertex p_2 on the right side (black dot, label to the right)",
    "Panel (a): vertex p_1 on the lower right (black dot, label below-right)",
    "Panel (a): candidate point p_i outside to the left (black dot, label to the left)",
    "Panel (a): darker gray triangle p_0–p_j–p_i attached along the left edge of Q_j",
    "Panel (a): thick gray boundary polyline tracing the enlarged hull p_0 → p_i → p_j → p_k → … → p_2 → p_1 → p_0",
    "Panel (a): the shared chord p_0–p_j separating the dark triangle from Q_j",
    "Panel (b): thick gray hull chain from p_0 rightward to p_1, up the right side through unlabeled dots, across the top through p_j",
    "Panel (b): vertex p_0 bottom left, vertex p_1 lower right, vertex p_j top, plus unlabeled boundary dots",
    "Panel (b): point p_i at upper left, point p_r just to its right, forming a thin-outlined light gray triangle p_i–p_r–p_0",
    "Panel (b): interior point p_t inside triangle p_i p_r p_0",
    "Panel (b): dashed segments p_i–p_t and p_r–p_t",
    "Panel (b): small arc at p_t marking the (nonleft) angle",
    "Highlight overlay: angle arc at the pivot vertex being tested",
    "Stack readout strip listing the current stack contents (added for the demo)",
    "Legend distinguishing 'hull boundary', 'candidate region', 'popped/interior point'"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "caseToggle",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "That the same geometric test has two outcomes: a left turn grows the hull (panel a), a nonleft turn traps a point strictly inside a triangle so it can never be a hull vertex (panel b).",
      "label": "Case",
      "options": ["(a) left turn — push", "(b) nonleft turn — pop", "both panels"],
      "default": "(a) left turn — push"
    },
    {
      "id": "scanStep",
      "type": "button",
      "teaches": "How Graham's scan incrementally maintains the hull: each candidate is tested against the top two stack points, then pushed or the offending point is popped.",
      "label": "Step the scan",
      "states": ["start", "test p_j p_i", "push p_i", "test p_i p_r p_t", "pop p_t", "done"],
      "default": "start"
    },
    {
      "id": "pointFocus",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "Which specific triple of points certifies a point's status — the two supporting hull vertices for an accepted point, or the three enclosing points for an interior point.",
      "teachesDetail": "Clicking p_i, p_j, p_r, or p_t highlights that point and its supporting triangle or hull edge."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The hull so far",
      "narration": "The light gray polygon Q_j is the convex hull of the points processed so far, with p_0 at the bottom and the stack running counterclockwise to p_j. Everything already accepted is a vertex of this shape.",
      "state": { "caseToggle": "(a) left turn — push", "scanStep": "start" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Testing a left turn",
      "narration": "The next candidate p_i is tested against the top two stack points. The turn from p_0→p_j to p_j→p_i is a left turn, so p_i lies outside the current hull.",
      "state": { "caseToggle": "(a) left turn — push", "scanStep": "test p_j p_i" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Push: the hull grows",
      "narration": "p_i is pushed onto the stack and the dark triangle p_0–p_j–p_i is annexed to Q_j. The boundary now detours through p_i, showing that a left turn strictly enlarges the hull.",
      "state": { "caseToggle": "(a) left turn — push", "scanStep": "push p_i" }
    },
    {
      "title": "A nonleft turn",
      "narration": "Switch to panel (b). Here the angle at p_t formed by p_i, p_t, p_r is not a left turn — the dashed segments and the arc show p_t bending the wrong way.",
      "state": { "caseToggle": "(b) nonleft turn — pop", "scanStep": "test p_i p_r p_t" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Pop: the point was never a vertex",
      "narration": "Because the turn is nonleft, p_t lies inside triangle p_i p_r p_0, whose three corners are all hull points. A point interior to a triangle of hull points cannot be a hull vertex, so p_t is popped.",
      "state": { "caseToggle": "(b) nonleft turn — pop", "scanStep": "pop p_t" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Two cases, one test",
      "narration": "Side by side, the panels show the invariant: every candidate either extends the boundary outward or reveals an earlier point as interior. Nothing else can happen.",
      "state": { "caseToggle": "both panels", "scanStep": "done" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "A nonleft turn is not just a bookkeeping rule — it is geometric proof that the middle point lies strictly inside a triangle formed by three other hull points, so it can never be a vertex of the convex hull.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Two side-by-side panels on a white background, labeled (a) and (b) in italic beneath each. Panel (a): a light gray filled convex polygon occupying the right two-thirds, its interior labeled 'Q_j' in italic serif; vertices marked as small filled black dots with italic serif labels p_0 (bottom left), p_j (top), p_k (upper right of p_j), two unlabeled dots along the upper-right chain, p_2 (right side), p_1 (bottom right). A medium/dark gray triangle p_0–p_j–p_i is attached on the left, with p_i protruding further left; the shared chord p_0–p_j is drawn as a thin line so the two shaded regions read as distinct. The outer boundary of the combined shape is a thick light-gray stroke. Panel (b): the same overall hull silhouette but unfilled — only a thick gray boundary chain from p_0 (bottom left) rightward to p_1, up the right side through unlabeled dots, and across the top through p_j. At the left sits a narrow light-gray triangle with thin black outline, corners p_i (upper left), p_r (to its right), p_0 (bottom); inside it a dot p_t with dashed segments to p_i and p_r and a small arc at p_t marking the angle. For the demo, use a saturated accent color (e.g. orange) for the currently tested angle arc and triple, and a muted red for a popped point; add a small stack readout strip under each panel showing stack contents bottom-to-top. Keep all labels italic serif in the style of CLRS."
}

